.TH REFSTRESS 1
.SH NAME
refstress \- what runs at the same time as what
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B refstress
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Refstress
puts sixteen processes on a single shared heap object and has each of
them assign and drop a reference to it forty thousand times, while
counting how many of them are inside a region at the same moment.
.PP
It began as an attempt to provoke a reference-count race and could not,
which turned out to be the more useful result: it measures why.
.SS What it reports
Two regions are counted and they answer differently:
.IP
.EX
region                                    processes inside at once
pure Dis instructions                     1, always
the same, with a host system call in it   4 to 16
.EE
.PP
Dis execution is
.IR serialised .
A process must hold the virtual machine's one execution slot to run, and
.B acquire
and
.B release
hand that slot to exactly one process at a time.
The extra
.B vmachine
threads exist to cover processes that are
.I waiting
on the host, not to run them side by side.
.PP
That distinction is easy to get wrong, and was.
Counting threads inside
.BR vmachine 's
call to
.B xec
gives seventeen, which looks like seventeen processes running Dis and is
not:
.B xec
spans the system calls a process makes through
.BR mcall ,
and a process inside a host call has already released the slot.
.SS Why the counter is unprotected
The shared counter it uses has no lock and is not atomic on purpose.
A lost update can only make the count too low, so the measurement errs
towards reporting
.I less
concurrency than there is \(em which is the safe direction for a test
that fails when it sees too little.
.SS What it asserts
That exactly one process executes Dis at a time, and that more than one
can be inside a region containing a host call.
Either changing is a significant change to the virtual machine, and it
should be noticed here rather than inferred later from something else
going wrong.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/refstress.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR fdstresstest (1),
.IR emu (1)
.PP
.B doc/hpc-plan.md
bug 8.
.SH BUGS
It cannot provoke the reference-count race it was built for, and now it
is clear that nothing written in Limbo can.
The compiled path's counter updates run only while holding the slot, so
they cannot race each other; what they can race is the C-side
.B incref
and
.B decref
in emu's own device code, on threads that hold no slot, and that is not
reachable from here.
.PP
The two regions are nested, so the outer count includes time spent in the
inner one.
That is harmless for what is asserted but means the two numbers are not
independent measurements.
.PP
Sixteen processes, one object, one shape of work, one host call.
